Q: Getting folder path with Python Tkinter tkFileDialog A: <p>I have created this class, which is going into a wider tkinter project. Class opens a standard dialog box, returns the directory path and the directory name.</p>
<pre><code>from tkinter import filedialog
import os

class GetDirectoryDynamically:
    path: str = &quot;&quot;
    dir_name: str = &quot;&quot;

    def select_directory(self) -&gt; None:
       self.path = filedialog.askdirectory()
       self.dir_name = os.path.basename(self.path) if self.path else ''

    def get_directory_path(self):
        return self.path

    def get_directory_name(self):
        return self.dir_name

gDD  = GetDirectoryDynamically()
gDD.select_directory()
dir_path = gDD.get_directory_path()
dir_name = gDD.get_directory_name()
print(f&quot;Selected directory: {dir_path if dir_path else 'No directory selected'}&quot;)
print(f&quot;Selected directory name: {dir_name if dir_name else 'No directory selected'}&quot;)
</code></pre>

Q: How to update or add fields to a sublist item (array?) A: <p>You are missing the payment_gateways layer: <code>payment_options</code> should be an object with property <code>payment_gateways</code>, as the <code>List</code>.</p>
<p>Zoho's error responses aren't very informative and you have to take some of them with a grain of salt.</p>
<p>When I see an error like:</p>
<pre><code>{&quot;code&quot;:1038,&quot;message&quot;:&quot;JSON is not well formed&quot;}
</code></pre>
<p>I interpret it as, <em>the JSON does not match what they are <strong>expecting</strong></em>. Looking at your payload, you lost the <code>payment_gateways</code> array (List in Deluge).</p>
<p>From your info statement:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;expiry_date&quot;:&quot;2024-11-25&quot;,
    &quot;Amount&quot;:&quot;407.0&quot;,
    &quot;accept_retainer&quot;:true,
    &quot;retainer_percentage&quot;:&quot;25&quot;,
    &quot;configured&quot;:true,
    &quot;PaybyCC&quot;:true,
    &quot;payment_options&quot;:[
        {
            &quot;configured&quot;:true,
            &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;:false,
            &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;:false,
            &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;:true,
            &quot;gateway_name&quot;:&quot;stripe&quot;
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>It should be:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;expiry_date&quot;:&quot;2024-11-25&quot;,
    &quot;Amount&quot;:&quot;407.0&quot;,
    &quot;accept_retainer&quot;:true,
    &quot;retainer_percentage&quot;:&quot;25&quot;,
    &quot;configured&quot;:true,
    &quot;PaybyCC&quot;:true,
    &quot;payment_options&quot;:{
        &quot;payment_gateways&quot;: [
             {
                &quot;configured&quot;:true,
                &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;:false,
                &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;:false,
                &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;:true,
                &quot;gateway_name&quot;:&quot;stripe&quot;
            }
        ]
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Deluge is not very friendly with building Maps and Lists; a good rule of thumb is to declare all your objects first and build from the inside out:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>quote = Map();
payment_options = Map();
payment_gateways = List();
payment_gateway = Map();

// don't include the newlines like I have below
// this is just for readability in the thread.
payment_gateway.putAll({
    &quot;configured&quot;: true, 
    &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;: false, 
    &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;: false, 
    &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;: true, 
    &quot;gateway_name&quot;: &quot;stripe&quot; 
});

payment_gateways.add(payment_gateway);
payment_options.put(&quot;payment_gateways&quot;, payment_gateways);
quote.put(&quot;payment_options&quot;, payment_options);
</code></pre>

Q: How can I get &quot;long&quot;, &quot;double&quot;, ... information from a RESTinio query from a REST API? A: <p>RESTinio provides built‑in type conversion for query string parameters (<a href="https://stiffstream.com/en/docs/restinio/0.7/expressrouter.html#casting-parameters" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RESTinio - Casting Parameters</a>).</p>
<p>You can use <code>cast_to&lt;T&gt;</code> like this:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;restinio/cast_to.hpp&gt;

if (qp.has(&quot;AcceptTime&quot;)) {
    long dAcceptTime = restinio::cast_to&lt;long&gt;(qp[&quot;AcceptTime&quot;]);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This can be even more simplified with the use of <a href="https://stiffstream.com/en/docs/restinio/0.7/valueor.html#value-or" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>value_or</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;restinio/value_or.hpp&gt;

long dAcceptTime = restinio::value_or&lt;long&gt;(qp, &quot;AcceptTime&quot;, 0);
</code></pre>
<p><code>value_or</code> returns a default value if <code>AcceptTime</code> key is missing. If the conversion fails, it will throw an exception.</p>
